# Two Judgments
Tamil title: இரண்டு தீர்ப்புகள்

Do ghosts and spirits exist or not?

This question is asked frequently, especially of Self-Respecters. Some years ago an actor friend told me: “Anna, everything you say is right. But on this matter of spirits alone I cannot accept your opinion. Spirits exist. I have seen one.”

Unlike some others, he genuinely cared for me, so his words were not meant to provoke an argument. They arose from his deep conviction. Many people like him say spirits exist and claim to have seen them.

If you ask what the spirit looked like, the answer is amusing:

“I heard some kind of noise. It seemed as though a light breeze was blowing. Then I saw something black. As I kept watching, my mind began becoming confused. The black colour then appeared like some kind of form. Suddenly there was a sound. My body began trembling and I broke into sweat. It felt as though somebody slapped me sharply. I cried ‘Alas!’, became faint and fell down.”

Most stories take this form. We cannot simply say that people deliberately lie. What would they gain by doing so? But after hearing them, we ought to ask certain questions. Usually we do not, because both speaker and listener already “believe” that spirits exist.

They say:

> It seemed like something.
> Some kind of sound was heard.
> Something black appeared.
> My mind became strange.

What does “something” mean here? The speaker does not know, and the listener cannot discover it. How can this become evidence?

It appeared to be water, but when I reached it there was none; at last I understood it was a mirage.

Something looked like a red fruit. I threw stones repeatedly, but it did not fall. My friend laughed and said, “That is not a red fruit. A green leaf and the adjacent tender shoot overlap and look like one, but it is not.” When I examined it closely, I saw he was right.

“Can you not tell from his gait that Kannan is coming?” my friend asked. “No, Kannan never wears an upper cloth. The approaching man is wearing one; it is Muthu, not Kannan,” I argued. When he came near, we saw that he was neither Kannan nor Muthu but Somu.

“Ah, what lovely flute music!” said one friend.

“No, it is a fiddle,” I said.

“Neither of you possesses the slightest musical knowledge; it is a gottu instrument,” said another. A fourth called it a clarinet. We walked toward the sound. As we approached, it seemed to all of us like a band. Nearer still, it became harsh. Then we laughed together: what had seemed in turn a flute, fiddle, gottu, clarinet and band was merely the sound of an oil press.

Have we not often spoken in this way about objects, forms, sounds and colours? In all these cases, there is only doubt and lack of clarity.

When that uncertainty joins fear, trouble begins. Instead of resembling a gottu or clarinet, if the press's sound seemed like a howl or growl, would one not fear a jackal or leopard? In the middle of a town, there might be no fear; outside it, fear would certainly arise.

If uncertainty and fear are joined by an idea already planted in the mind, trembling naturally follows. If the oil press sounds like a growl, one hears it outside the village, and one has previously heard that a leopard roams there, fear must arise.

This is where the spirit is born.

Uncertainty, surroundings, fear and an existing idea combine, and a spirit is born.

One may ask why the mistake is not later discovered, as one discovers that a mirage is not water. Reconsider the story told by those who saw a spirit and the answer appears. They say: “Immediately my body went cold; I fainted and fell.” Because their eyes closed and thought ceased, they had neither time nor condition of mind to determine whether it was a spirit.

An incident has now given much work to those who dismissed all these arguments as mere Self-Respect talk. The issue entered a court. A sub-judge had to rule whether spirits exist.

A murder case was being tried. Counsel argued that the dead man had not been killed by another person but that some spirit had struck and killed him. People ordinarily say, “A ghost slapped him; he vomited blood from nose and mouth and died.” Similarly, counsel maintained that no human being killed the victim in this case; a spirit did it.

The judge who began trying a murder case thus acquired a new question: whether spirits exist. He delivered a sound judgment.

In a village in Chittoor district, a man named Senganna Gounder was murdered. His body was found half a mile from his house. A servant called Gundudu, who had accompanied him when he left home, said that a spirit appeared as they neared the village; that he became frightened and ran home; and that Sengannan was caught by it. Counsel argued that the spirit beat the man to death.

District Judge A. S. P. Aiyar ruled:

> Village people commonly believe in ghosts and spirits. Even if such belief exists, the court cannot accept it and hold that a ghost committed this murder. If it did, every murderer would soon try to place the blame upon a ghost, and courts could render no service to the public.

This was appropriate for protecting the proper operation of law. But the judge did not stop there:

> The question whether ghosts and spirits truly exist arose in other countries too before human civilization developed. With civilization's advance, this belief gradually disappeared.

He explained that people believed in spirits during days when ignorance and darkness occupied the mind, and that enlightenment removed the superstition in other countries. He then described the condition here after the world had advanced:

> But in India alone, such superstitions still continue.

Observe the plural “superstitions.” Belief in spirits is not the only one; many others exist. Even after the world advances and civilization causes ignorant beliefs to disappear and truth to shine, superstitions persist here alone. That is astonishing. But the judge mentioned something still more astonishing and bewildering:

> In such matters, I am surprised that even lawyers argue that a ghost or spirit may have committed the crime. How can we regard these educated men as having advanced beyond uneducated villagers?

A murder of knowledge worse and more cruel than the murder under trial takes place here. Its “criminals” not only escape; no law exists under which to prosecute them.

Knowledge says Earth is round; the Puranic preacher says it is flat. We cannot prosecute those committing this murder of knowledge.

Science explains an eclipse; people murder that knowledge, say Rahu swallows the moon, immerse themselves in a tank and recite spells. No court can try this intellectual murder, because the law contains no provision.

Knowledge says people are equal. Others say caste exists and commit another murder of knowledge. These killers not only go unpunished and untried; astonishingly, they are praised as noble ones, holy ones, acharyas, sages, elders, scholars and artists.

In days containing so much murder of knowledge, we rejoice that this judge not only made a rational statement about spirits but also lashed educated people who behave worse than ordinary villagers. We welcome this sound judgment.

Its lesson must be understood: in such matters, educated people are worse than the uneducated. At least many ordinary people, despite believing in spirits, say, “Even a spirit cannot harm me. It frightens us only if we fear it; if we look at it courageously, it will run away.” Educated people do not even speak with this courage. They seek a magician.

We ask the people to examine this judgment carefully. Do not imagine that only Self-Respect platforms say ghosts and spirits do not exist. A court has now delivered that very judgment.

## The Second Judgment

“Even if he is stone, he is the husband; even if he is grass, he is the man.”

“From the day we extended our neck for the marriage pendant, we became subject to him. Is that not its meaning?”

“She is his property. Must she not obey whatever he says?”

“Whether he is a drunkard, madman, womanizer or tyrant, what does it matter? He is her husband, and she must submit.”

“She must eat if he feeds her; otherwise she must become a corpse at his feet. What other path exists for household goddesses?”

Are these not our country's “justice” and “fairness”?

A legal question has now arisen about this too.

Village elders say: “The Self-Respecter talks a thousand kinds of nonsense. He even says it is wrong for a wife to submit to her husband. He says women should receive rights, and that a husband must act according to her wishes. If she dislikes him and he torments her, apparently she may even establish a separate household. Such are the things these Self-Respecters say.”

Samakkal was a young woman. During three years in her husband's house, she suffered many cruelties. She tried reasoning, lamenting, pleading with her husband, weeping that she could not endure such cruelty at her young age, praying and appealing. Nothing succeeded. The arrival of a co-wife further complicated her plight.

Her husband, Chinna Paiya Goundan, seemingly believed that, according to our country's “justice” and the elders' “law,” she had no path but to remain in his house despite cruelty, beating and kicking. When Samakkal sought the law's aid, the question assumed another form.

The Dharmapuri District Munsif held that Samakkal could live separately; that she was not wrong to refuse life with a cruel husband who had married another woman; and that the husband must pay her five rupees each month.

The case went to a higher court. Salem District Judge Chowdhury affirmed the lower court and offered sound observations on a wife's rights in family life:

> A husband cannot be permitted to act as he pleases upon the sole belief: “Whatever happens, my wife must live with me; she can survive only through me; this is the proper order.” He must make life civilized in a way that gives her a desire to live with him.
>
> He cannot be allowed to say: “The marriage has occurred; therefore, as husband, I possess the right to compel her to live with me.” When a husband cannot assure her a reasonably good life, the wife may live separately and he must bear the expense. He cannot escape that duty.

Cruel husbands had advanced all kinds of doctrines. Aunts and grandmothers cited stale tales. Whenever women suffered without even time to wipe their tearful eyes, people sobbed “fate, fate.” It was not fate but the trouble caused by believing empty talk.

We said that a wife is not her husband's slave but his companion in life; that no warmth can exist in a family if a husband regards his wife as property; and that women must not be enslaved. Elders replied, “This is how Self-Respecters talk. Is not a woman's virtue to endure whatever her husband does?”

The judgment says otherwise. We call attention to it.

The Samakkal case occurred in this country. So did the spirit case.

We now ask comrades who condemned, mocked and abused the Self-Respecters' words to think a little. The Self-Respecter spoke; the law now says the same thing.

*13 April 1947*

---NOTES---
The work presents two court decisions as legal confirmation of Self-Respect arguments: one rejects supernatural causation in a murder case, and the other recognizes a mistreated wife's right to separate residence and maintenance.

`சூனாமானா` is a colloquial distortion or hostile nickname for `சுயமரியாதை` (Self-Respect), used by opponents of the movement and reclaimed ironically here. `கல்லென்றாலும் கணவன்; புல்லென்றாலும் புருஷன்` is a patriarchal proverb meaning that a husband must be accepted regardless of worth. `கிரஹலட்சுமி` idealizes the wife as the goddess of the household.

The instrument OCRed as `கோட்` or `கோட்டு` may refer to the *gottuvadyam*. The servant's name `குண்டுடு`, Judge A. S. P. Aiyar's initials, Samakkal's name and Judge Chowdhury's spelling are retained conservatively from the source. A fragment `(5-1` at the end of Image 8 appears to be damaged page furniture or a broken date and is not used in translation. Several lines on Images 5-6 are visibly corrupt, but their surrounding reasoning is recoverable; the Tamil source remains unchanged.
